  "response_text": "The stack allocation method we used works by treating the stack memory as a fixed-size region. We first obtain the value of MSP_INIT_VAL which tells us where the main stack starts in memory. We can then identify the value of the process stack (PSP) by subtracting a fixed amount of memory (0x400 bytes) from the main stack\u2019s starting address. This works because the stack grows downward in memory so allocating new stack space means moving to a lower address. \\n\\nwe need to know the initial location of the MSP so that we can determine where the stack begins and make sure that the PSP stack doesn\u2019t overlap with the main stack. Knowing the MSP allows us to ensure proper memory separation for multitasking and threading management.\\n",
